From: AuraLite OS OpenGL work
Subject: [PATCH G7] OpenGL phase G7 — vertex arrays, VBOs and display lists

Phase G7 of GL_PLAN.md, on top of G6.  Geometry can now be submitted in bulk
instead of one glVertex call at a time.

  * src/glarray.c -- client vertex arrays with arbitrary stride and eight
    component types, glDrawArrays/glDrawElements/glArrayElement, and the
    GL 1.5 buffer-object subset for both targets.
  * src/gllist.c -- display lists, GL_COMPILE and GL_COMPILE_AND_EXECUTE.
    A list stores a COMMAND LOG, not a captured vertex batch, so matrix and
    state commands inside it replay at call time -- which is what the
    specification defines and what applications rely on.
  * tests/unit/test_glarray.c -- 36 checks.  The equivalence tests compare the
    ENTIRE framebuffer against the immediate-mode equivalent, because a stride
    or attribute-ordering bug shows up as a few pixels a spot check misses.

An honest negative result: vertex arrays are NOT faster here.  10 000
triangles cost 4.5 ms immediate vs 4.6 ms via glDrawArrays; with rasterisation
removed entirely, 3.2 ms vs 3.5 ms.  The per-vertex transform dominates, so
removing a call per vertex is noise.  A separate bulk path that inlined the
transform would duplicate the pipeline and risk the two paths disagreeing
about lighting or clipping, so it was rejected; this is documented at the top
of glarray.c so nobody "optimises" it back in.

Also fixes: test_glarray was in LIBGL_TESTS but missing from UNIT_TESTS, so it
was built but never run.

Known pre-existing issue surfaced by the longer test: under -smp 2, roughly
one /gltest run in three fails a different arbitrary check; under -smp 1 the
same binary passes 150/150 three runs running.  That matches the kernel's
documented BSP-only scheduling limitation, not libgl, which is single-threaded.

Verified from a clean rm -rf build: make test-unit 59/59, /gltest 150/150 in
QEMU, test_opengl.sh 71/71, /glcube "clean exit, 12 frames", no regressions.

